course: geography
lesson: 56
title: Soil
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Soil is not just dirt. It is a living mix of tiny bits of rock, rotting leaves and animals called humus, water, air and countless living things, and almost all our food grows in it.
explain: Dig a deep hole in a garden and you see layers: dark crumbly topsoil, paler subsoil under it, then broken rock, and at the bottom solid bedrock. These layers are called horizons.
explain: A handful of healthy soil holds more living things than there are people on Earth, mostly bacteria and fungi, while earthworms mix it, let in air and water, and pull dead leaves down to rot.
explain: Soil grows painfully slowly: a few centimetres can take hundreds to a thousand years. Yet it can be washed or blown away in one bad storm, and about a third of the world's soils are already damaged.
fact: Soil is a mix of tiny rock pieces, rotting plant and animal matter called humus, water, air and living things.
fact: It can take hundreds to a thousand years to form just a few centimetres of soil.
fact: Soil has layers called horizons: dark topsoil, paler subsoil below, and broken rock above the bedrock.
fact: A handful of healthy soil contains more living things than there are people on Earth, mostly bacteria and fungi.
fact: Earthworms mix soil, make tunnels for air and water, and turn dead leaves into rich soil.
fact: Soil texture depends on how much sand, silt (grains finer than sand) and clay it has; clay holds water and drains badly, sand drains at once, and loam, a balanced mix, is the best for farming.
fact: The black soils of Ukraine and southern Russia, called chernozem, are among the most fertile in the world, which is why Ukraine is called the breadbasket of Europe.
fact: Many rainforest soils are surprisingly poor, because heavy rain washes nutrients out and most nutrients are stored in the plants.
fact: Farmers add nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium to soil as fertiliser to grow more food.
fact: About a third of the world's soils are degraded by erosion, salt, pollution or overuse.
fact: Soils hold more carbon than all the world's plants and the air put together.
exercise: What is soil made of? | Rock bits, humus, water, air and living things | Only crushed rock, ground fine by wind and weather | Only rotting leaves | Only sand and rainwater, mixed together
exercise: What is humus? | Rotting plant and animal matter in soil | A kind of hard rock found under the soil | A type of earthworm | Salt left behind by the sea long ago
exercise: How long can it take to form a few centimetres of soil? | Hundreds to a thousand years | A single afternoon of heavy rain | About one week, after ploughing | About one year of rotting leaves
exercise: What are soil horizons? | The layers of soil from topsoil down to bedrock | The lines in the distance where the sky meets the land | Rows of crops in a field | Cracks made by frost
exercise: Which layer is usually darkest and richest? | Topsoil | Deep subsoil | Broken rock | Solid bedrock
exercise: What lies at the very bottom, under all the soil layers? | Bedrock | More topsoil | Water only | Hot lava
exercise: How many living things are in a handful of healthy soil? | More than there are people on Earth | About ten, mostly worms | None, soil is dead and empty of life | About a hundred, if it is wet
exercise: Which living things are most of those in soil? | Bacteria and fungi | Rabbits and moles in burrows | Ants and spiders | Snails and slugs under the leaves
exercise: How do earthworms help soil? | They mix it, make air tunnels and turn dead leaves into soil | They eat all the roots so the plants have to grow faster and stronger | They harden the soil into rock | They pump out all the water
exercise: What decides soil texture? | How much sand, silt and clay it has | How many earthworms live in it each year | How deep down the bedrock is under it | How hot and dry the summer is each year
exercise: Which soil is best for farming? | Loam, a balanced mix of sand, silt and clay | Pure clay, which holds water like a swimming pool | Pure sand, which drains at once | Bare gravel with no humus
exercise: What is chernozem? | The very fertile black soil of Ukraine and southern Russia | A red desert sand in central Australia that nothing grows in | A frozen soil in Antarctica | A salty soil by the sea
exercise: Why are many rainforest soils poor? | Heavy rain washes nutrients out and plants store most of them | They have far too little rain to let anything rot down into soil | They are too cold for bacteria | They are made of pure gold
exercise: Which three nutrients do farmers add in fertiliser? | Nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium | Gold, silver and copper, ground fine | Oxygen, helium and neon | Salt, sugar and chalk from the shop
exercise: About how much of the world's soil is degraded? | About a third | Almost none | All of it | About one in a million fields
exercise: What holds more carbon than all plants and the air together? | Soils | Clouds | Oceans' foam | Deserts' sand
test: kid at school: "soil's just dead dirt, nothing lives in it lol" — what's true? | A handful of healthy soil has more living things than people on Earth | He's right, soil is lifeless | Only worms live in soil, nothing else at all, and only a handful of them | Soil only lives in spring
test: People cut down a rainforest to farm, but after a few years the crops fail. Why? | The soil was poor; most nutrients were in the trees | Rainforest soil is too rich for crops and burns their roots | The crops were scared of the jungle | It rained too little in the rainforest
test: A builder digs a deep hole. Which order of layers does he most likely see from top to bottom? | Topsoil, subsoil, broken rock, bedrock | Bedrock, subsoil, topsoil, broken rock | Subsoil, bedrock, topsoil, broken rock | Broken rock, topsoil, bedrock, subsoil
test: What would happen to a lawn if all its earthworms vanished? | Soil would get packed and airless, and dead leaves would rot slower | Grass would grow twice as fast with nobody eating its roots underground | Soil would turn into rock at once | Nothing would change at all
test: Why was Ukraine called "the breadbasket of Europe"? | Its black chernozem soils are among the most fertile in the world | It has the most rain in the world | It has no soil, so wheat grows on rock | It is covered in rainforest
test: grandad: "lose the topsoil in a storm, it's back next spring, no worries" — is he right? | No, a few centimetres can take hundreds to a thousand years to form | Yes, soil grows back every spring once the worms wake up and get going | Yes, rain makes new soil in weeks | No, soil can never form at all
test: Sam's garden soil turns into a puddle that won't drain after rain. What does it probably have too much of? | Clay | Rich humus | Air spaces | Earthworm tunnels
test: Why might protecting soil help fight global warming? | Soils hold more carbon than all plants and the air | Soil makes the Sun weaker | Soil cools the air like a fridge | Soil soaks up sunlight and stores it as ice for winter
test: A farmer's field grows weaker every year. What does she most likely add? | Fertiliser with nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium | Pure salt from the sea | Sand from the beach only | Nothing, since soil never runs out
test: Which is a way soil gets degraded? | Erosion, salt, pollution or overuse | Earthworms digging lots of tunnels | Leaves falling and rotting on top | Rain soaking in slowly
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the composition of soil? | A mixture of mineral particles, humus, water, air and living organisms | A homogeneous substance consisting solely of crushed rock | A deposit consisting exclusively of decayed vegetation | A mixture of sand and sea water alone
exam: Identify the principal reason for which numerous rainforest soils are deficient in nutrients. | Heavy rainfall leaches nutrients, the greater part being retained within vegetation | Rainfall in such regions is wholly insufficient | Temperatures are too low for decomposition | Such soils consist entirely of bedrock
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning loam. | It is a balanced mixture of sand, silt and clay most suitable for agriculture | It is a soil consisting wholly of clay that does not drain | It is a soil consisting wholly of gravel | It is the bedrock beneath the subsoil
exam: Ascertain the approximate duration required for the formation of a few centimetres of soil. | Several hundred to one thousand years | Several days following a period of heavy rainfall | A single season of cultivation | Several million years
